reference "LAB REFERENCE\nPhysics you can inspect.\nBuilding an experiment\n\nDrag or click a component in the library. Click an electrical terminal, then another electrical terminal to wire them. Click the orange heater port and the orange tank port to immerse the element. Double-click a switch to operate it. Select a wire and press Delete to remove it.\n\nDrag the grid to select a group. Hold Space to pan; scroll to zoom. Use V for Select, H for Pan, W for Wire, R to rotate, Ctrl/Cmd D to duplicate, Ctrl/Cmd C/V to copy and paste, and Ctrl/Cmd Z to undo.\n\nPhysical construction and shared machines\n\nSwitch between Schematic and Physical without resetting. Physical geometry uses metres and radians, with a separate pan/zoom camera. Use the geometry tools, Shift-selection, 0.1 m snap, alignment, rotation/resize handles and undo. Select two bodies, choose a joint, then click world anchors. A gear/rack couples two grounded bearings or slides. Build mode ends when simulation time advances; Reset restores initial poses and unlocks geometry.\n\nm = ρ A depth · Idisc = ½mr² · τ = Iα · Espring = ½kΔx²\n\nPlanck 1.4.2 provides Box2D rigid-body collision, friction, restitution, sleeping and continuous collision detection. Static, dynamic and prescribed-velocity kinematic bodies are supported. Joints connect static/dynamic bodies; kinematic joint drive is deferred. Material density/contact coefficients affect mechanics. Material heat capacity and conductivity are metadata; visual contact creates no thermal connection or damage. Engineering equipment uses static housings; attach a separate dynamic wheel through a grounded bearing and the motor shaft selector.\n\nThe motor’s MNA current and torque drive the actual rigid-body shaft. Wheel speed determines back EMF and pump load; motor rotor inertia is included once in the body mass matrix. Mechanical substeps are at most 1/480 s with bounded implicit electrical/shaft iteration; explicit springs/dampers may require smaller steps. Joint constraint drift and the first-order rigid-body integrator remain measured numerical errors. External forces and impulses are recorded energy sources; brake, contact and stop losses leave as heat. They do not automatically warm an unrelated thermal component.\n\nOpen the mechanism, rotor, descent, lift or rotational-control starters in Physical tools. Add position/speed sensors to the existing control graph; external force actuators account for their work. Challenges 007–009 score actual descent, lifting and changing-load regulation. A tank’s hydraulic elevation follows spatial y only when explicitly linked; pipe length remains configured independently. Moving finite equipment, spatial water, fracture and automatic contact conduction are unsupported.\n\nDC electrical model\nV = IR    P = VI = I²R    Σ Inode = 0\n\nModified nodal analysis solves voltages and ideal source currents using partial-pivot Gaussian elimination. Wires have zero resistance. Switches use 0.01 Ω when closed and infinite resistance when open. Lamps and heaters use constant resistance; filament temperature dependence is omitted. A voltmeter loads the circuit with 10 MΩ.\n\nDisconnected islands each have a local reference. Ground components share one reference. Shorts across ideal sources and singular source configurations pause the simulation. Ratings exceeded for 0.5 s cause passive elements to fail open. Fuses open after 0.1 s over rated current. Supply current limits pause the solver; they do not implement current regulation.\n\nCoupled thermal model\nCh dTh/dt = P − UAhw(Th − Tw) − UAha(Th − Ta)\nmcp dTw/dt = UAhw(Th − Tw) − UAwa(Tw − Ta)\n\nThe heater and well-mixed water tank are separate thermal masses. The full coupled thermal matrix uses backward Euler at 1/60 s. Water density is fixed at 1 kg/L and cₚ = 4,180 J/(kg·K). Electrical power is updated before the thermal solve. Every passive electrical component stores dissipated heat and loses heat to a 20°C ambient. Source internal losses are omitted.\n\nConduction is energy-conserving between connected nodes; backward Euler introduces first-order time discretization error. Ambient loss is integrated using end-of-step temperature. Tanks support liquid water from 1–95°C; crossing these limits pauses the experiment. Boiling, phase change and wall thermal mass remain unsupported. Moving water uses the hydraulic and conservative advection models below.\n\nHydraulic networks and finite water storage\nΣQ = 0 · Δp = f(L/D)ρv|v|/2 · pbottom = ρgV/A\n\nBlue terminals connect fluid nodes; insert pipe components to create physical resistance. A pipe’s configured length and diameter determine its losses independently of the canvas route. Water uses ρ = 1,000 kg/m³, μ = 0.001 Pa·s, cp = 4,180 J/kg·K. Laminar friction uses 64/Re, turbulent friction uses Swamee–Jain, blended across Re 2,300–4,000. A damped nodal Newton solve has bounded iterations and monitored continuity residuals.\n\nTanks are finite, vented, well-mixed vessels with bottom connections. Their elevation and water level set pressure potential. The engine splits timesteps at empty/full inventory events, then blocks outward/inward boundary flow and resolves the network. Full tanks do not overflow; sealed tanks are explicitly unsupported. Pipe and junction inventory is zero: thermal transport is instantaneous and conserves mass-weighted enthalpy. Pipe travel time, water hammer, cavitation, viscosity changes and spatial mixing are deferred. Pipe heat loss uses explicit thermal UA; material and insulation are descriptive metadata.\n\nMotors and centrifugal pumps\nI = (dV − keω)/R · τ = ktI · J dω/dt = τ − τload − bω\nH = H0[(ω/ωrated)² − ½(Q/Qrated)²] · Phyd = ρgHQ · Pshaft = Phyd/η + cω³\n\nConnect a motor’s gray shaft terminal to a pump shaft and wire its electrical terminals to a real circuit. Motor back EMF is stamped into MNA. The ideal optional PWM drive exchanges bus current dI and armature voltage dV, preserving electrical power; speed inputs use RPM. Shaft speed is solved implicitly at the midpoint with pump load feeding back into current. Internal substeps are at most 1/240 s. Armature inductance, commutation and drive losses are omitted. A pump includes a reverse-flow check. Beyond the positive-head curve it acts as a passive restriction; turbine behavior is unsupported and reported.\n\nCopper and rotational friction heat the motor; pump losses and hydraulic dissipation heat flowing water. At zero flow, pump drag heat goes to the environment. The energy panel separates internal transfers from electrical supply, thermal/rotor/gravitational storage and external losses. Hydrostatic storage splitting has a measured timestep error; the engine reports a residual beyond 0.5 J or 0.5% supplied energy. This is a teaching model, not equipment certification.\n\nHydraulic automation, saves and challenges\n\nPressure sensors output Pa, flow sensors m³/s, level sensors percent. They read calculated state and share the existing controller graph, fault semantics and simulation clock. A PI regulator has bounded output and anti-windup; motor speed commands use RPM and valve position commands percent. Actuated valves have a finite stroke time and fail position; actuator power is omitted. Independent source and high-level sensors can gate motor enable without relying on the primary level measurement.\n\nUse the water-transfer, automatic-supply and heated-loop starters in the library. Challenges 004–006 start with an unwired scaffold and score the calculated performance; hydraulic scored runs lock operating edits after startup to require autonomy. Normal Save includes recorded inputs and elapsed time for a live run; Import recomputes all dynamic state using recorded fixed steps. Export scenario configuration saves only the initial model. Replay and restore yield to the browser, support two simulation hours, and do not trust imported dynamic scores.\n\nAutomation and independent protection\n\nPurple connections carry typed numeric or Boolean signals and no electrical power. Each fixed step reads sensors, evaluates the acyclic control graph in dependency order, applies relay contacts, solves DC power, integrates coupled thermal state, checks ratings, and records results. Temperature lag uses an exact first-order update; optional noise uses a repeatable seeded generator. Hysteresis turns on below its lower threshold and off at the upper threshold. Timers use simulation seconds.\n\nThe thermostat combines the same sensor and hysteresis models. The safety cutoff independently measures its assigned thermal mass, latches open at its threshold or on invalid sensing when configured, and requires a manual reset below its reset margin. Relay and cutoff contacts have finite resistance and thermal capacity. Arcing, bounce, coil energy, and real safety certification are outside this model. A welded contact ignores its command.\n\nA cooling jacket adds UA(T − Tcoolant) to the attached mass. Its coolant is an ideal infinite reservoir, with signed heat included in external energy exchange. There is no circulating fluid, pump, refrigeration power, or phase-change model.\n\nMeasurement and construction\n\nEach trace targets a component or an explicit node/reference pair. Floating islands cannot be compared electrically; the result is invalid. Mixed units use separate labeled plots. Hover a chart to read exact recorded values; time-window and history controls also select the energy-balance interval. CSV exports all configured probes with blank cells for unavailable measurements. Stored energy is shown separately for water, elements, and other electrical masses; internal immersion heat is not double-counted.\n\nSelected wires expose draggable orthogonal bend points. Electrical crossings never connect; branching requires shared terminals or explicit junctions. Connected duplication and saved assemblies remap internal wiring and sensor targets; external connections and targets are omitted.\n\nReference calculation\n12 V / 6 Ω = 2 A   ·   12 V × 2 A = 24 W\n10 kg × 4,180 J/(kg·K) × 60 K = 2,508,000 J\n\nAt 2 kW, the theoretical water-only heating time is 1,254 s. The experiment also heats the element and loses energy to ambient, so completion takes longer.\n\nClock, measurements, and project files\n\nSimulation advances in fixed 1/60 s steps independently of rendering. Speeds range from 0.25× to 4×. Advance 60 s executes 3,600 ordinary steps while paused. Long background frames are bounded to 0.25 s of wall time, so the clock slows safely when a tab is suspended. Telemetry records at 4 Hz, retaining the latest 14,400 samples. Events use simulation timestamps. The Event log offers deterministic replay from the last reset and recorded editor inputs, up to two simulation hours. Replay reconstructs the final state on the pinned engine; cross-platform bitwise identity is not guaranteed. It does not provide timeline scrubbing.\n\nSave downloads a version 4 spatial or version 3 schematic scenario or a live-run archive and stores a recovery copy on this device. Import opens a validated project file. Project files contain construction and initial conditions; loading starts a reproducible fresh experiment. CSV exports measured telemetry. Mass, inventory, model variants, and other physical configuration changes require Reset once time has advanced. Operating controls remain live. Version 1 files migrate through validated defaults.\n\nChallenge scoring\n\nMandatory objectives, safety limits, and budgets must all pass. Score = 60 + 20η + 10(1 − cost / budget) + 10τ. For heating, η = min(1, 2,508,000 / consumed joules), τ = min(1, 1,254 / elapsed seconds). For First Light, η = 1 and τ = min(1, 3 / elapsed seconds). Thermal Runaway requires 240 seconds of regulation with at least 97% in-band time, 2–80 relay transitions, actual independent interruption below 95°C, a manual safety reset, and 120 seconds of stable recovery. Its score is 50 + 15 stability × overshoot factor + 10 energy efficiency + 10 economy + 10 time efficiency + 5 switching economy, with the factors clamped to 0–1. Coolant capacity halves after 120 regulation seconds; after at least 240 seconds and a full automatic switching cycle, control relay contacts weld and cooling fails. Following 30 seconds of verified isolation these faults are repaired, while the safety latch remains tripped. One star for completion, two at 80, three at 90."
